Ahhh good catch matt

```
	// IPBlock defines policy on a particular IPBlock. If this field is set then
	// neither of the other fields can be.
```

So sounds like, since IPBlock is being set, the podSelector is simply not taking effect .

@cmluciano , are we missing some validation in the APIServer here?

Yes we do validation testing for this [here](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/3a110546ed8ab319a3997b788889c943cdb6f05d/pkg/apis/networking/validation/validation_test.go#L391-L437).

I tried on master to add the following test as a success case to attempt to simulate this 
```golang
{
			ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{Name: "foo", Namespace: "bar"},
			Spec: networking.NetworkPolicySpec{
				PodSelector: metav1.LabelSelector{
					MatchLabels: map[string]string{"a": "b"},
				},
				Egress: []networking.NetworkPolicyEgressRule{
					{
						To: []networking.NetworkPolicyPeer{
							{
								PodSelector: &metav1.LabelSelector{},
								IPBlock: &networking.IPBlock{
									CIDR:   "192.168.0.0/16",
									Except: []string{"192.168.3.0/24", "192.168.4.0/24"},
								},
							},
						},
					},
				},
			},
		},
```

This fails as expected with `Expected success for 15, got [spec.egress[0].to[0]: Forbidden: may not specify both ipBlock and another peer]`

When I grab the yaml and try locally on a kind cluster built from master it does fail validation. This does make sense though because if we look at the validation code [here](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/e648deca3bc72e3f233ead213dd03d4229ff986a/pkg/apis/networking/validation/validation.go#L81-L106) we only check for if podSelector is nil.

All of this said, I agree that using namespaceSelector seems like the more appropriate PeerType based on the comments.

